1
00:00:00,120 --> 00:00:04,980
Module 5, Video 5.1. What must be in place before the blocks can call each other?

2
00:00:06,240 --> 00:00:09,760
Right. So before one block can actually call another across ministries,

3
00:00:10,480 --> 00:00:13,920
uh, each really must be a member of the data exchange layer,

4
00:00:14,620 --> 00:00:19,480
and each service registered with its contract and, you know, access granted to the caller.

5
00:00:20,140 --> 00:00:27,360
Exactly. When we evaluate the reference architecture in your ministry, we often encounter, well, assumptions about interoperability.

6
00:00:27,820 --> 00:00:33,680
So if your minister is told the new registration service will, uh, reuse identity in the learner register,

7
00:00:34,300 --> 00:00:35,460
what is your job to check?

8
00:00:36,160 --> 00:00:42,980
Well, your job is to check whether those calls can happen at all, because across ministries, it is never just an automatic process.

9
00:00:43,420 --> 00:00:44,440
Right. It takes some setup.

10
00:00:44,800 --> 00:00:49,980
Yeah, exactly. Three distinct elements must be in place first, and, you know, each one has an owner.

11
00:00:50,480 --> 00:00:52,500
And those controls are pretty central to the infrastructure.

12
00:00:52,680 --> 00:00:58,500
So to understand how that governs your program, what are the three things the information mediator specification asks for?

13
00:00:58,980 --> 00:01:04,200
Okay, so number one, a member, meaning an organization is admitted to the data exchange layer.

14
00:01:04,519 --> 00:01:06,940
Right. Number two, a registered service.

15
00:01:07,580 --> 00:01:09,160
Which means publishing the contract.

16
00:01:09,540 --> 00:01:13,020
Yeah, the provider publishes its contract, an OpenAPI description.

17
00:01:13,440 --> 00:01:16,660
It outlines exactly what data it requires and what it returns.

18
00:01:17,240 --> 00:01:20,560
So callers can program precisely to those technical boundaries.

19
00:01:20,860 --> 00:01:23,260
Exactly. And number three, a grant.

20
00:01:24,000 --> 00:01:26,380
The owner of the service explicitly allows the caller.

21
00:01:27,220 --> 00:01:31,400
XRoad, which is the software behind Progress' exchange, works exactly this way.

22
00:01:31,940 --> 00:01:33,120
It relies on all three then.

23
00:01:33,740 --> 00:01:37,160
Yep. It needs all three components to physically permit a transaction.

24
00:01:37,880 --> 00:01:41,460
That level of strict control really defines the architecture, but, uh,

25
00:01:42,200 --> 00:01:46,480
does the specification impose a rule that every call must go through the exchange?

26
00:01:47,100 --> 00:01:53,640
Uh, well, it is strongly recommended across the internet, but it's not actually required between blocks on a single platform.

27
00:01:54,040 --> 00:01:54,680
Okay, got it.

28
00:01:55,340 --> 00:02:03,340
Progressive makes the choice to use the exchange, though, because its blocks belong to different authorities, and each must control who reads its data.

29
00:02:04,100 --> 00:02:08,900
Makes sense, because without that controlled communication, data just becomes, like, duplicated, right?

30
00:02:09,300 --> 00:02:12,880
Yeah, duplicated, rekeyed, and incredibly costly to change.

31
00:02:13,740 --> 00:02:16,060
So let us apply that to the current state of the platform.

32
00:02:16,640 --> 00:02:20,340
Who exactly are the members on Progress' exchange linkup today?

33
00:02:20,780 --> 00:02:24,980
Well, the Digital Government Authority, PDGA, owns and operates it,

34
00:02:25,600 --> 00:02:27,620
and PNEA acts as the caller.

35
00:02:28,260 --> 00:02:37,500
Right. Then the Learner Registry, PLR, publishes one enrollment service, and the Identity Authority, PNIA, publishes one identity read service.

36
00:02:37,980 --> 00:02:40,560
And currently, only PNEA may call either of those.

37
00:02:40,880 --> 00:02:44,980
Exactly. And the Ministry of Education, MOISE, is not a member at all.

38
00:02:45,520 --> 00:02:51,020
Okay, so since MOISE isn't a member right now, what four things does this course add to the configuration?

39
00:02:51,580 --> 00:02:56,480
Number one, a member for the registration service with its application registered on the exchange.

40
00:02:57,520 --> 00:03:02,500
Number two, a write service on the learner register registered with its OpenAPI contract.

41
00:03:03,220 --> 00:03:06,360
And that step two contract dictates how the data's formatted, right?

42
00:03:07,020 --> 00:03:07,740
Yeah, precisely.

43
00:03:08,380 --> 00:03:12,560
Then number three, grants, letting the registration service call that write service,

44
00:03:13,220 --> 00:03:14,560
and the identity service as well.

45
00:03:15,300 --> 00:03:16,580
Got it. And the fourth?

46
00:03:17,020 --> 00:03:20,860
Number four, the payments block joins as a member, publishing its own interface.

47
00:03:21,260 --> 00:03:24,060
That interaction raises a critical point for the reference architecture.

48
00:03:24,680 --> 00:03:29,040
How does the system prove that simply being on the exchange is not permission?

49
00:03:29,560 --> 00:03:33,740
Well, the registration and write services are listed on the network, sure.

50
00:03:34,580 --> 00:03:39,360
But if a member without a specific grant makes the call, the exchange just intercepts it.

51
00:03:39,480 --> 00:03:40,540
Like at the network layer?

52
00:03:40,900 --> 00:03:41,120
Yeah.

53
00:03:41,980 --> 00:03:45,220
It refuses the request entirely within access denied fault.

54
00:03:45,480 --> 00:03:46,860
So it's extremely strict.

55
00:03:47,160 --> 00:03:47,840
It has to be.

56
00:03:48,480 --> 00:03:51,940
Before any plan says that two blocks will talk, check three things:

57
00:03:52,580 --> 00:03:56,360
membership, a registered contract, and a grant from the owner.

58
00:03:57,020 --> 00:03:59,100
If one is missing, the call fails.

59
00:03:59,620 --> 00:04:00,980
Sources are in the video description.
